.TH VIDEOSYNTH 1
.SH NAME
videosynth \- Sandin Image Processor-style video synthesizer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dreammachines/videosynth
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Videosynth
is a digital work-alike of Dan Sandin's Image Processor (1973), the
subject of a chapter in Nelson's
.IR "Computer Lib/Dream Machines" :
a patchable rig of oscillators, colorizers, and feedback loops. There
is no real video signal to patch here, so a waveform generator (three
interfering sine terms) drives an HSV colorizer (hue cycling over
time), and a software feedback loop blends each frame with the last so
patterns trail and decay rather than cutting cleanly frame to frame.
.PP
The image is a deliberately chunky, low-resolution grid (each logical
cell a solid block of real screen pixels), both an authentic period
choice and what keeps the expensive part of the computation \(em the
sine terms \(em running once per cell instead of once per pixel.
.PP
Keys:
.B c
cycles through four built-in "patches" (oscillator/colour parameter
sets, standing in for re-patching the real machine's cables),
.B f
toggles the feedback loop,
.BR + / -
adjust the animation speed,
.B r
resets, and
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/dreammachines/videosynth.b
